4. Create a secret key

For example, in application.properties:

jwt.secret=your-very-long-secret-key
jwt.expiration=3600000

3600000 milliseconds = 1 hour.

In a real production system, don't hard-code a secret into source control. Use an environment variable or secret manager.




5. Create JwtService

This class will be responsible for:

generating JWT
extracting username
validating JWT


@Service
public class JwtService {

    @Value("${jwt.secret}")
    private String secret;

    @Value("${jwt.expiration}")
    private long expiration;

    private SecretKey getSigningKey() {
        return Keys.hmacShaKeyFor(
                secret.getBytes(StandardCharsets.UTF_8)
        );
    }

    public String generateToken(String username) {

        return Jwts.builder()
                .subject(username)
                .issuedAt(new Date())
                .expiration(
                        new Date(System.currentTimeMillis() + expiration)
                )
                .signWith(getSigningKey())
                .compact();
    }

    public String extractUsername(String token) {

        return Jwts.parser()
                .verifyWith(getSigningKey())
                .build()
                .parseSignedClaims(token)
                .getPayload()
                .getSubject();
    }

    public boolean validateToken(String token, String username) {

        String extractedUsername = extractUsername(token);

        return extractedUsername.equals(username)
                && !isTokenExpired(token);
    }

    private boolean isTokenExpired(String token) {

        Date expirationDate = Jwts.parser()
                .verifyWith(getSigningKey())
                .build()
                .parseSignedClaims(token)
                .getPayload()
                .getExpiration();

        return expirationDate.before(new Date());
    }
}



6. What happens when generating JWT?

When we call:

jwtService.generateToken("afham");

Spring creates something conceptually like:

HEADER
{
   "alg": "HS256"
}

and

PAYLOAD
{
   "sub": "afham",
   "iat": "...",
   "exp": "..."
}

Then:

Header + Payload
       +
   Secret Key
       ↓
   Signature

Finally:

HEADER.PAYLOAD.SIGNATURE

is returned.




7. Now create Login API

Suppose we have:

@RestController
@RequestMapping("/auth")
public class AuthController {

    private final AuthenticationManager authenticationManager;
    private final JwtService jwtService;

    public AuthController(
            AuthenticationManager authenticationManager,
            JwtService jwtService) {

        this.authenticationManager = authenticationManager;
        this.jwtService = jwtService;
    }

    @PostMapping("/login")
    public String login(@RequestBody LoginRequest request) {

        Authentication authentication =
                authenticationManager.authenticate(
                        new UsernamePasswordAuthenticationToken(
                                request.getUsername(),
                                request.getPassword()
                        )
                );

        return jwtService.generateToken(
                authentication.getName()
        );
    }
}

LoginRequest:

public class LoginRequest {

    private String username;
    private String password;

    // getters and setters
}




